robustness: rather than chase, check whether seq 4 reverts. Monitor a few times over ~30s while the turn continues (I have no sleep in execute, but shell can sleep). Let me check after doing the cleanup verification, with a short sleep.\n\nAlso: the fact that seq 4 had 10 chars at token time suggests the revert happened. Let me test more concretely: is there another user row (seq 18) whose text is 146 chars — that''s the second message (\"Oops! That random string...\") — fine, no token (146 chars, doesn''t contain it).\n\nNow: after my replacement, the app might revert. To make the redaction durable, ideally:\n- Edit and then verify after the session goes idle. The session writes at message completion. My edits happened mid-turn; the app may rewrite on each message commit for the whole session? Hmm.\n\nLet me test: run a marker occurrence check with *numeric* output only (count of rows whose text length is 10 — the secret''s length). If any row has a text field of length 10, that''s suspicious (only the first user message had it). Let me check across all rows: lengths of `json_extract(data,''$.text'')` where type=''user''.\n\nThen, to be safe, do a final scrub right at the end of this turn, and tell the user: the app may restore the value if it rewrites the session; recommend restarting OpenCode (or that I can re-run the scrub). Hmm, unsatisfying.\n\nBetter idea: find where the source of truth is. OpenCode V2 uses event sourcing: `event` table + session_message. If the app rebuilds session_message from events, editing session_message alone is futile. Let me check the event table''s data for this session — inspect a couple of rows, and check `type` values. And check whether event.data contains message text (with the secret now absent → look for any event data containing \"text\" fields).\n\nLet me look at event rows for aggregate ses_e222... and see types, plus grep for the second user message text (seq 18, 146 chars) in event data — if present, then events store mes